

🜂 TIME MODULE INTERFACE

(the C. Matthew Blackwell standard)

This interface defines how a time model plugs into the engine, how it updates fields, how it computes experienced time, and how it interacts with the meta‑equation and organisms.

Every time model — classical, relativistic, warp, discrete, crystalline — must implement this.

🜁 Interface: TimeModel

interface TimeModel {
    init(context)
    compute_experienced_time(observer, local_state) -> Δτ
    update_fields(dt, local_state, context) -> new_state
    invariants(local_state) -> set<Invariant>
    regime_signature() -> TimeRegimeSignature
}

🔥 Breakdown of each component

Below is the meaning and purpose of each method — and why it matters for your meta‑equation and organisms.

1. init

Purpose:Initialize internal parameters, caches, or precomputed structures.

Why:Some time models need precomputation (warp kernels, metric inverses, dilation maps).

2. compute_experienced_time

Signature:

compute_experienced_time(observer, local_state) -> Δτ

Purpose:Given an observer’s state and the local time fabric, compute the actual experienced time increment.

Examples:

Classical:(\Delta\tau = \Delta t)

Relativistic:(\Delta\tau = \sqrt{-g_{\mu\nu} u^\mu u^\nu}, \Delta\lambda)

Warp-like:(\Delta\tau = \Phi(\Theta, g, u), \Delta\lambda)

This is the heart of the engine — the thing that makes time felt.

3. update_fields

Signature:

update_fields(dt, local_state, context) -> new_state

Purpose:Evolve the local fields (Θ, g, ρ, Ξ, etc.) according to the model’s rules.

This is where:

dilation spreads

compression collapses

warp gradients propagate

discrete ticks jump

time crystals oscillate

frozen pockets lock

This is the PDE engine hook.

4. invariants

Signature:

invariants(local_state) -> set<Invariant>

Purpose:Return the invariants this time model enforces.

Examples:

Classical:

uniform flow

no curvature

Relativistic:

metric compatibility

lightcone structure

Warp:

energy-density bounds

stability thresholds

Discrete:

integer tick conservation

These invariants feed directly into the meta‑equation.

5. regime_signature

Signature:

regime_signature() -> TimeRegimeSignature

Purpose:Provide a compact fingerprint of the model’s identity.

This is how the meta‑equation knows:

what regime is active

what transitions are allowed

what organisms can attach to it

what stability rules apply

Think of it as the genetic code of the time model.

🜄 Supporting Types

TimeRegimeSignature

struct TimeRegimeSignature {
    name: string
    dimensionality: int
    continuity: enum { CONTINUOUS, DISCRETE, FRACTAL }
    causal_structure: enum { CLASSICAL, RELATIVISTIC, WARP, NONE }
    stability_class: enum { STABLE, META_STABLE, UNSTABLE }
}

Invariant

struct Invariant {
    name: string
    value: float | tensor | symbolic
}

